  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{\u2032}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{\u2032}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+6-2{x}^{2}+4x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{10} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$",
